<WikiLayout title="WARDOGS Wiki Sources" description="Source standards and primary references used by the WARDOGS Wiki knowledge database." canonicalPath="/sources/" pageType="WebPage">
  <h1>WARDOGS Wiki Sources</h1><p>The Wiki prioritizes official publisher and storefront material, then clearly labels community-observed mechanics when primary documentation is incomplete. A source must support the claim beside it; a related page is not treated as evidence for an unrelated number.</p>
  <h2>Primary references</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.team17.com/games/wardogs" rel="nofollow">Team17 WARDOGS page</a> — publisher overview, features and official media.</li><li><a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/1867240/WARDOGS/" rel="nofollow">WARDOGS on Steam</a> — release status, official descriptions and published updates.</li></ul>
  <h2>Evidence hierarchy</h2><ol><li>Official publisher documentation and patch material.</li><li>Official storefront descriptions and announcements.</li><li>Direct in-game observations tied to a documented version.</li><li>Community observations that are clearly labeled and independently checkable.</li></ol><p>Third-party databases can help locate a topic, but they are not automatically treated as authoritative. Unsupported values are marked <strong>Currently unconfirmed</strong>.</p>
  <h2>Version handling</h2><p>Facts that can change are tied to the documented snapshot. Entity pages include patch history so readers can see which release context the record represents. When a patch invalidates a value, the entry should be revised rather than silently mixing multiple versions.</p>
  <h2>Media standards</h2><p>Images should identify the relevant entity or explain a system. Watermarked promotional edits, unrelated screenshots and third-party logos are not used as substitutes for evidence. Generated database illustrations are decorative and are labeled as illustrations through their alt text and captions.</p>
  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What happens when sources disagree?</h3><p>The conflict is documented and the value remains <strong>Currently unconfirmed</strong> until stronger evidence resolves it.</p><h3>Can community research be included?</h3><p>Yes, when it is clearly labeled, scoped to a version and not presented as an official statement.</p>
</WikiLayout>
